WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.070 --> 00:00:08.070
Building on the last video, let's look at the scenario using the provided project named Bear CP.

2
00:00:08.070 --> 00:00:21.940
It shows point cloud data of a bear model processed from both top and bottom.

3
00:00:21.940 --> 00:00:29.300
In essence, it's the exact scenario and the exact workflow that you had tried with the line in the last video.

4
00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:36.299
Yet, after scanning both sides and creating masks, which gets you to this point where the project was saved,

5
00:00:36.299 --> 00:00:44.280
aligning the cameras again, as you did before, doesn't work in this instance.

6
00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:52.829
Instead of ending up with a single component and a single finalized point cloud,

7
00:00:52.829 --> 00:00:56.829
you still get two unfused components of the bear.

8
00:00:56.829 --> 00:01:03.469
When this happens, you need to help the process a little bit by using control points.

9
00:01:03.469 --> 00:01:10.469
Control points are points that you explicitly identify on multiple components to help bring parts together.

10
00:01:10.469 --> 00:01:21.379
There are usually points of high contrast, but in this case, things were made easier with pieces of tape.

11
00:01:21.379 --> 00:01:28.379
Select component 0, representing the top of the bear. You'll target the left hind leg first.

12
00:01:28.379 --> 00:01:33.629
From the Alignment tab, choose Add Control Points.

13
00:01:33.629 --> 00:01:43.620
As you hover over the left hind leg, rays appear in the 3D view, representing the cameras that can see that point.

14
00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:47.620
Click a point in that area. You don't need to be accurate at this time.

15
00:01:47.620 --> 00:01:55.540
Right-click to end the command. A new point named Point 0 has been created.

16
00:01:55.540 --> 00:02:00.829
Several images that can be used to track this point have also been identified.

17
00:02:00.829 --> 00:02:05.829
Select the first image in that list. This switches you to a three-pane layout.

18
00:02:05.829 --> 00:02:09.860
Point 0 can now be seen in both the 3D and 2D views.

19
00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:19.219
In the 2D view, you need to relocate Point 0 to the reference point with a click and drag to bring it to the corner of the red triangle.

20
00:02:19.219 --> 00:02:26.080
Once this is done, you can do the same with the other images, but here's a trick.

21
00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:33.530
As you are relocating the image reference with a click and drag, do not release the mouse button.

22
00:02:33.530 --> 00:02:41.990
Use the down arrow key to move to the next image in line. This can be a great time saver.

23
00:02:41.990 --> 00:02:51.740
Once you have a few references in place, you need to pinpoint them on the pictures taken from the bottom half.

24
00:02:51.740 --> 00:02:56.740
So select component 1, representing the bottom half of the bear.

25
00:02:56.740 --> 00:03:07.870
Also, and this is crucial, you want to ensure that Point 0 is selected in the list, otherwise hitting add control points would actually create a new point.

26
00:03:07.870 --> 00:03:15.159
And orbit around to view the left hind leg. This may be a bit harder to visualize as the bear is upside down.

27
00:03:15.159 --> 00:03:24.740
With that done, Point 0 selected that is, use add control points and click a point on the left hind leg on the upside down model.

28
00:03:24.740 --> 00:03:33.340
Again, right-click to exit the command. A few more reference images have been added to the Point 0 list.

29
00:03:33.340 --> 00:03:46.270
Go ahead and adjust them as you did before, and keep in mind that as you relocate them, other images may be identified.

30
00:03:46.270 --> 00:03:51.270
Once you go through this process, chances are there may be some hits and misses.

31
00:03:51.270 --> 00:03:57.460
The misses are represented by red or yellow icons, basically errors or warnings.

32
00:03:57.460 --> 00:04:04.939
You could try and reposition them, but often, you typically have more than enough reference points that you can remove the bad ones.

33
00:04:04.939 --> 00:04:12.680
Once you have done so, and there are no more reported errors, you can move on to the next point.

34
00:04:12.680 --> 00:04:21.939
For that, you may want to start again with component 0, but more importantly, you don't want Point 0 to be selected in the list.

35
00:04:21.939 --> 00:04:30.939
You actually want to create a new point this time around, so make sure Point 0 is unselected by selecting any image reference underneath.

36
00:04:30.939 --> 00:04:38.949
Let's move to the left front leg. Add a control point there near the green mark.

37
00:04:38.949 --> 00:04:46.750
A new point, Point 1, is created, and you can repeat the procedure to adjust the reference points.

38
00:04:46.750 --> 00:05:07.750
Once done, on the upper half of the bear, select the bottom component and make sure Point 1 is selected.

39
00:05:07.750 --> 00:05:14.819
Again, you want to identify Point 1 on the bottom half of the bear, so it's important you have the point selected this time.

40
00:05:14.819 --> 00:05:26.360
Use Add Control Points one more time to add references to the bottom half, and adjust them as you did before.

41
00:05:26.360 --> 00:05:42.379
You now have two control points, identified on the two halves of the model, on one side of the bear.

42
00:05:42.379 --> 00:05:46.899
The more control points you have, the better success you will achieve.

43
00:05:46.899 --> 00:05:54.990
There are four color dots on this model, but three of them should be enough for a good triangulation, so let's do one more.

44
00:05:54.990 --> 00:06:03.730
Again, select component 0 and make sure Point 1 is not selected by selecting one of its reference images.

45
00:06:03.730 --> 00:06:17.730
Add a new control point, perhaps for the right-hand leg near the white marker.

46
00:06:17.730 --> 00:06:47.620
Right-click to end the command. Re-align the reference images and clean up any errors or warnings.

47
00:06:47.620 --> 00:07:14.329
When done, select component 1 and make sure Point 2 is selected this time.

48
00:07:14.329 --> 00:07:20.329
Use Add Control Points to identify image references for that point on the bottom half of the bear.

49
00:07:20.329 --> 00:07:46.620
Adjust the positioning as you have learned to do.

50
00:07:46.620 --> 00:07:52.620
At this stage, you should have enough information with three control points to rebuild the point cloud.

51
00:07:52.620 --> 00:07:57.670
You can switch back to a 2D pane layout.

52
00:07:57.670 --> 00:08:05.149
To build the final solution, you only need to use the Merge Components tool in the Alignment tab.

53
00:08:05.149 --> 00:08:11.790
This will use the information you have set up using control points to fuse the two halves of the bear together.

54
00:08:11.790 --> 00:08:18.889
It will create a new component, component 01 in this case, that you can adjust to your liking.

55
00:08:18.889 --> 00:08:46.759
I would suggest the usual, using Set Ground Plane to reorient it, meshing it with normal detail,

56
00:08:46.759 --> 00:09:06.340
perhaps decimating the triangle count and smoothing it, and of course texturing it before you are ready for export.

57
00:09:06.340 --> 00:09:16.139
As you orbit around, you will see that the process worked perfectly.

58
00:09:16.139 --> 00:09:21.309
The last video will be a bit of a bonus.

59
00:09:21.309 --> 00:09:30.309
In it, you learn how you can re-process a model in RealityCapture after you have manipulated it in a GCC application.
